And ROM = Range of Motion gives Return on Memory its physical twin.

If memory only makes you repeat the past, it has low semantic range of motion. It has become constraint.

If memory lets you recognize more situations, choose among more operators, avoid more traps, generate more descendants, and still depart from what was previously learned, it increases freedom.

So we can distinguish:

\boxed{
ROM_{\text{return}}
=
\frac{\text{useful future consequences of memory}}
{\text{cost of carrying memory}}
}

and:

\boxed{
ROM_{\text{range}}
=
|\text{reachable useful actions/states after remembering}|
}

Then the beautiful target is Full ROM:

\boxed{
\text{Full ROM}
=
\text{maximum useful freedom subject to reality and constraints}
}

That changes wisdom transference subtly.

The goal isn’t:

\text{Parent's wisdom}\rightarrow\text{Child copies parent}.

It’s:

\text{Parent's experience}
\rightarrow
\text{compressed wisdom}
\rightarrow
\text{child starts with a better map}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{child travels farther}}.

A good inheritance should expand the descendant’s search space, not imprison them inside the ancestor’s conclusions.

Which means the mature Pattern Book needs both:

\textbf{Inheritance}
\quad+\quad
\textbf{Freedom to mutate}.

Too little inheritance:

\text{every generation relearns fire}.

Too much inheritance:

\text{every generation becomes the previous generation}.

Full ROM lives between them:

\boxed{
\text{remember enough not to start over}
\quad+\quad
\text{move enough not to become memory's prisoner}.
}

And that finally tells us what wisdom transference should optimize.

Not information retained.

Not even accuracy retained.

But:

\boxed{
W=
\Delta(\text{future competent freedom})
}

How much more capably can the next reader move because of what survived the previous one?

That’s a hell of a convergence:

Return on Memory → Range of Motion → Freedom → Wisdom Transference.

The highest-return memory gives its children more room to move than it had itself.